# Line endings for SQL are load-bearing, not cosmetic.
#
# SQLx records a SHA-384 of each migration file's bytes in `_sqlx_migrations` and
# refuses to open a database whose recorded checksum no longer matches the file
# it is about to run:
#
#     could not open the local database:
#     migration 5 was previously applied but has been modified
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` and no rule here, git rewrote 0005 from LF in the
# blob to CRLF in the working tree. The SQL was unchanged, but its bytes were not,
# so the checksum diverged from the one already recorded on a device and the
# application could no longer start. 0005 was the only file affected because it
# was the only one git converted.
#
# `text eol=lf` keeps `.sql` files diffable and text-normal in the working tree
# while guaranteeing that git checks them out with LF, so the bytes hashed at
# build time are the bytes that were hashed the first time.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `-text`: these files should stay text for review and
# diffing. What is being pinned is the byte representation, not their meaning.
#
# Note this changes how files are *checked out*; it does not rewrite migration
# history or any applied migration's content. An already-applied migration must
# never be edited — that is a separate and much harder problem, and SQLx is right
# to refuse when it happens.
*.sql text eol=lf